statement.p:
Find the arc length of the graph of the function 
f(x)=3 \sqrt{x^{3}}
from x= 5 to x=8.

solution.p:
And the sum of lifting all of the slices from the bottom (x=0) to the top of the water level's depth (x=2) is
\begin{array}{cl}
\int_0^{2}(9.8)(1070)\left({\frac{4}{25}} \pi x^2\right)(5-x)\,dx  \amp  =10486\left({\frac{4}{25}}\right)\pi\int_0^{2} (5 x^2-x^3)\,dx \\
  \amp    =10486\left({\frac{4}{25}}\right)\pi\left[\frac{5 x^3}{3} - \frac{x^4}{4}\right]_0^{2}  \\
  \amp    =10486\left({\frac{4}{25}}\right)\pi\big[{\frac{40}{3}}-4\big]  \\
  \amp    =10486\left({\frac{4}{25}}\right)\pi\big({\frac{28}{3}}\big) \\
 \amp  \approx 49194\;\;\text{J}
\end{array}

statement.p:
Compute the value of the following improper integral.  If it is divergent, type "Diverges" or "D".
\int_{0}^{2}\! \frac{dx}{x^{2}-6x+5}
Answer:

solution.p:
Then, since \displaystyle\int \frac{r\sec^2{\theta}
\hbox{d}\theta}{(r^2(1+\tan^2{\theta}))^{(3/2)}} = \displaystyle\int
\frac{r\sec^2{\theta} \hbox{d}\theta}{(r^2(1+\tan^2{\theta}))(r^2(1 +
\tan^2{\theta}))^{(1/2)}} = \displaystyle\int
\frac{\hbox{d}\theta}{r^2\sec{\theta}} = \frac{1}{r^2} \sin{\theta}
using a trig identity.

statement.p:
Which of the given functions is a solution to \frac{dy}{dt}=k(y+A)?




y = A + C e^{kt}




y = -A + C e^{kt}




y = A^{-1} + C e^{-Akt}




y = -A + C e^{-kt}




y = A^{-1} + C e^{Akt}




y = A + C e^{-kt}

Find an equation of the curve that satisfies 
\frac{dy}{dx} = 30 yx^{5} 
and whose y-intercept is 4.

