statement.p:
Note that when the constant in the denominator is not 1, we still can use the geometric series, but we have to multiply by
a form of 1 that helps us.  We'll use \frac{\frac{1}{5}}{\frac{1}{5}}.
\frac{1}{(5+x)}=\frac{\frac{1}{5}}{\frac{1}{5}(5+x)}=\frac{\frac{1}{5}}{1+\frac{1}{5}x}=\frac{1}{5}\cdot \frac{1}{1+\frac{1}{5}x}

static.solution:
\displaystyle c_n = \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}, e.g. 



\displaystyle c_0 = \frac{f(0)}{0!} = 0. 



\displaystyle c_1 = \frac{f'(0)}{1!}; 



\displaystyle f'(x)=8 \arctan(6 x) + 8 x\frac{1}{(6 x)^2+1}6 
= 8 \arctan(6 x) + \frac{48 x}{36 x^2+1}; 



c_1 = 0.



\displaystyle c_2 = \frac{f''(0)}{2!}; 



\displaystyle 
f''(x) = \left( 8 \arctan(6 x) + \frac{48 x}{36 x^2+1} \right)' 



\displaystyle = 
\frac{48}{36 x^2+1} + \frac{48(36 x^2+1)-48 x36\cdot 2x}{(36 x^2+1)^2}



\displaystyle
= \frac{1728 x^2 + 48}{(36 x^2+1)^2} + \frac{48 - 1728 x^2}{(36 x^2+1)^2}
= \frac{96}{(36 x^2+1)^2};



\displaystyle c_2 = \frac{96}{2} = 48;



and so on. Another way is to use the power series for arctan:  



\displaystyle \arctan(t) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (-1)^n \frac{t^{2n+1}}{2n+1} 
= t-\frac{t^3}{3}+\frac{t^5}{5}-\frac{t^7}{7}+....



Then 



\displaystyle 8 x \arctan(6 x) = 
8 x \sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n \frac{(6 x)^{2n+1}}{2n+1} = 
\sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n \frac{8\cdot 6^{2n+1}}{2n+1} x^{2n+2}
\displaystyle  
= 48 x^2 - \frac{1728}{3} x^4 + \frac{62208}{5} x^6 - \frac{2239488}{7} x^7+.... 



So c_0=0, c_1=0, c_2=48, c_3=0, c_4=-\frac{1728}{3}=-576...



This is an alternating series. It converges if and only if 
\displaystyle \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{8 \cdot (6 x)^{2n+1}}{2n+1}=0. 
This limit is equal to 0 if and only if |6 x| \le 1, 
so \displaystyle |x|\le \frac{1}{6}. 
Therefore the radius of convergence is \displaystyle \frac{1}{6}.
